WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.510 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  some days  it was a sunrise so bold and golden it  didn't seem real  another it was a bottle of champagne a  young mom handed me  near the edge of crater lake the next  discovering a cooler someone had left  out near the edge of lava fields in  southern oregon  filled with the makings of rippier  floats  these were moments i experienced as a  backpacker on the pacific crest trail  and moments that i quickly learned  hikers dubbed trail magic  these were moments that connected me to  natural beauty and to the new friends  that i was hiking with  moments that brought me into the present  and sparked a grateful  deep breath yes i have hundreds of  brutal miles ahead of me  for right now i'm thankful for this  moment  when i came off the trail and returned  to my laptop i wondered  could i find the same feeling surrounded  by responsibilities and the daily grind  could this feeling be scaled across  spaces communities and careers  figuring this out is important because a  lot of us here  are trying to solve hard problems for me  that's solving climate change but  whether we're solving a structural  problem  or just taking on the challenges of life  it can be  an emotional exhausting slog  that requires a touch of this magic to  get through  today i want to share with you first the  elements that make up trail magic  and the positive psychology behind this  feeling  second why trail magic isn't just for  hikers  and third how we can create more of this  for ourselves  and for others this curious phenomenon  has its roots in america's oldest  backpacking communities  every year since the 1950s hundreds of  backpackers have trekked  2600 miles from mexico to canada on the  pacific crest trail  the pct for four months  they hike 30 miles a day across snow  packs deserts and towns  my sister was crazy enough to do this  and i was crazy enough to  follow her for a month for us this  adventure meant  long days and endless blisters it meant  eating cold mashed potatoes and tuna on  repeat  it meant weathering injuries and  hitchhiking into towns  but i quickly noticed that amidst these  challenging lows  hikers made the most of the highs  they received any beautiful moment in  nature or  unexpected act of kindness with a  heightened sense of wonder  and gratitude trail magic  one moment while i was hiking captured  the essence of this  it was somewhere in the timberline  wilderness and we were up at 4  and hiking by 5am with headlamps and a  plan to hike 27  miles that day but around 1 pm we  stumbled upon a middle-aged couple  grilling by the trail instead of  powering on we spent hours  in the present eating burgers and  hearing about their retirement plans  this experience had the four hallmarks  of trail magic  it connected me to others it shifted my  perspective  into the present it sparked gratitude  and it surprised me connection  perspective gratitude and surprise  this magic may sound ephemeral but it's  actually neurological  trail magic is built on gratitude which  researchers from ucla  and upenn have proven increases  serotonin  dopamine and pro-social behavior in  short the appreciation of trail magic  makes us happy  it sparks a jolt of neurotransmitters  which increase  our well-being and our empathy  surprisingly trail magic also changes  our perception of time  how many of you have felt like your time  is precious  or in short supply see some nods and  same and we're not alone according to a  recent gallup poll of a thousand  americans  almost half felt that they didn't lack  or that they lacked enough time  in daily life the good news is that  researchers have found  that experiencing awe changes our  perception of time  both awe and trail magic are  characterized by a wonder  and gratitude that brings perspective  and as we shift into the present  psychologically  time stretches it makes us feel like we  have  more time available some of you are  saying  i love the outdoors i'll look for this  next time i'm there  but i bet a few of you are thinking i am  the opposite of outdoorsy  c'est la vie no magic for me but i  disagree  according to the environmental  protection agency the average american  spends 93  of her time indoors or in a car  but we can find trail magic during our  time outside and during  all of this time inside  when i finished my 500th mile my dad  picked me up on the side of the highway  the dirt had bled  permanently into the white trim of my  black running shorts  muscles i didn't know i had were sore  but i was radiant  i felt strong having completed a section  of the pct  and privileged to have that opportunity  i felt more connected than ever to the  oregon landscape  and to the crazier hikers with crazier  names that i had met along the way  fancy mac macgyver coconut  blasting oldies and flying down i-5 i  saw the bars grow  on the edge of my phone as we came back  into service range  as i went to call my partner alex a text  came through  can we talk and i knew  the car at 70 miles per hour stood still  and my heart froze as my mind spun  into a kaleidoscope of the pain and  hospital appointments  coming for the person i loved the most  his cancer was back  the levity and perspective i'd felt  earlier that very morning  seemed as distant as the oregon cascades  in our rearview mirror  trail magic felt ephemeral and rapidly  evaporating  but over the next year i was surprised  to find it again  in the friendship that we built with our  nurse dana  in the time the hospital snacks  spontaneously upgraded to graham  crackers  and saving our favorite show to watch  every week during alex's treatment  being open to these small moments of  magic and holding on tight  gave us a mini umbrella in the rain and  the energy  to take on the next year  what i'm saying is that i found trail  magic off the trail and it was powerful  a temporary antidote to worrying about  the future  a pause in relentless productivity a  celebration  in miniature of what matters  i remember when i was a kid and disney  was gospel and embraced magic like my  best friend  someone told me you have the power to  fly just jump and i did  but actually my sister told me that if i  jumped out of a tree with an umbrella  i could float just like jiminy cricket  so i jumped  and the ground came fast as i grew up i  learned skepticism and  injustice believing in magic felt  indulgent next to so many adult worries  and systems to dismantle  and as a productive professional i  didn't have time for it  but i missed that self that found wonder  in everything  and delight in the small things  i'm asking us to be open once again to  that feeling  of magic specifically the connection  perspective gratitude and surprise  of trail magic  surprising ourselves is tricky but  i'll give a few tactical ideas for how  we can discover this phenomenon  more often for ourselves first  celebrate those small moments the  unexpected burgers or the hospital  snacks  second take a risk and initiate a new  connection  maybe it's in a zoom chat or an uber or  the checkout line of a grocery store  i have to say that after a few years in  consulting i'd forgotten some of this i  used to fly every week for work  and i remember using every second on the  on the plane to fervently make slides  the pinnacle of efficiency but one  monday  mid-flight my laptop battery died and i  looked up  i started chatting with my seat mate a  hawaiian volcanologist  two hours later i had learned basic  volcanic geology  and the names of her kids jennifer and  sam  so the secret to finding trail magic  more often for yourselves  celebrate the small moments and  channel your six-year-old point of view  at this point a few of the entrepreneurs  in the audience might be asking  themselves  so can this idea scale and the answer is  absolutely and exponentially we can  amplify trail magic by creating it for  others  i already see this happening in our  community and i would love to see it  happen more  one friend then owed me money for a  drink while i was on vacation  just because another  wrote holiday notes to her favorite  baristas  trail magic doesn't have to be  complicated or  expensive just unexpected and  compassionate  the possibilities are endless but i'll  share a few of my favorites  if a friend mentions an important date  that's coming up  put a reminder on your calendar to reach  out then  treat the person behind you in line at  koopa to coffee  professionally unprompted think of two  people on your network with shared  interests that you think  would hit it off let's make this common  practice  starting today think of a gesture  have a rip your float sometimes grab  your umbrella and your six-year-old  point of view  the world will be a bit more magical  because you did

